/* Palo Alto Networks NGFW (Next-Generation Firewall)
   Parsing rule: catch-all no-hit drop route for `vendor="panw", product="ngfw_cef"`.

   HAND-CURATED, not mirrored from demisto/content — PAN-OS parsing and modeling are
   NATIVE to Cortex, so there is no upstream pack to fetch. This file is a local
   reference reconstruction for reverse-engineering, not a deployed rule.

   Do NOT route synthetic traffic through this INGEST block. `no_hit=drop` means an
   event that fails its parse is silently DISCARDED, and `panw_ngfw_cef_raw` has no
   modeling rule bound to it, so nothing that lands there can ever map to xdm.*.

   There is NO mechanism that fans one CEF stream out into the six per-log-type
   datasets by reading `log_type`: INGEST blocks route, and [MODEL: dataset=X] blocks
   only BIND a mapping to a dataset X that data already reaches by some other path.
   (An earlier version of this comment claimed such a fan-out existed. It does not.)

   To reach a specific dataset, use the DERIVED route — an unclaimed vendor/product
   pair whose `<lower(vendor)>_<lower(product)>_raw` equals the target:
     panw/ngfw_traffic       -> panw_ngfw_traffic_raw
     panw/ngfw_threat        -> panw_ngfw_threat_raw
     panw/ngfw_url           -> panw_ngfw_url_raw
     panw/ngfw_filedata      -> panw_ngfw_filedata_raw
     panw/ngfw_globalprotect -> panw_ngfw_globalprotect_raw
     panw/ngfw_hipmatch      -> panw_ngfw_hipmatch_raw
   Only `ngfw_cef` is claimed below, so each pair above derives cleanly and the
   native modeling rule — which binds by dataset name only — still applies. */

[INGEST:vendor="panw", product="ngfw_cef", target_dataset="panw_ngfw_cef_raw", no_hit=drop]
